<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of La Arada</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1851">1851</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of La Arada was fought on 2 February 1851 near Chiquimula in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>. Guatemalan forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rafael-carrera">Rafael Carrera</a> routed a larger allied army from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-salvador">El Salvador</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honduras">Honduras</a> that had invaded to overthrow him and restore the Central American federation. The victory secured Carrera's conservative regime and Guatemala's independence as a separate republic.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rafael-carrera">Rafael Carrera</a> returned from exile in 1849, the Salvadoran ruler Doroteo Vasconcelos gave asylum to Guatemalan liberals, who attacked the Guatemalan government through a newspaper run by José Francisco Barrundia, while Vasconcelos supplied money and weapons for a year to the rebel faction La Montaña in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>. By late 1850 he resolved on open war. Of the governments he invited to join him, only that of Juan Lindo in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honduras">Honduras</a> accepted, and on 4 January 1851 the two signed an alliance at Ocotepeque.</p><p>On 28 January Vasconcelos demanded that President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mariano-paredes">Mariano Paredes</a> give up power to a head of state chosen by the alliance, that Carrera be exiled, and that Salvadoran troops be free to occupy Guatemalan territory. The Guatemalan government answered that he had no authority under Salvadoran law to declare war and would treat the invaders as seditious.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The allies, about 4,500 men under Vasconcelos with the French soldier Isidoro Saget as chief of staff and divisions led by José Santos Guardiola, Ramón Belloso, José Trinidad Cabañas and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gerardo-barrios">Gerardo Barrios</a> among others, entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> at three points. Carrera, with 2,000 men, feigned a retreat to ground he had chosen and fortified the foothills of La Arada above the San José river, leaving 500 men in Chiquimula, so that 1,500 Guatemalans faced the invaders.</p><p>The allies attacked at half past eight in the morning of 2 February. They were thrown back once, took the first line of trenches and were driven out, and on the third assault the fighting became a melee under heavy Guatemalan artillery fire. Carrera then had the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> cane around the meadow set alight, trapping the allies between the fire, the guns and the river. Their centre broke, and the retreat became a rout.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The allies lost 528 dead and 200 prisoners, with 1,000 rifles and seven artillery pieces. Carrera crossed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-salvador">El Salvador</a> and occupied Santa Ana before Paredes recalled him, as the allies were asking for a ceasefire and a peace treaty. On 19 October 1851 a National Constituent Assembly enacted the Acta Constitutiva de la República, a brief text of 18 articles setting out the principles of the republic formed four years earlier. The battle also marked the beginning of Carrera's position as president for life.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of La Arada” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Conflicts with Guatemala and Honduras continued after 1841: Rafael Carrera defeated a Salvadoran and Honduran army at <b>the Battle of La Arada</b> in 1851, and in 1863 he besieged and took San Salvador, overthrowing Gerardo Barrios.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-el-salvador">Republic of El Salvador · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Briefly driven into exile in 1848, he returned in 1849, defeated a Honduran and Salvadoran invasion at the <b>Battle of La Arada</b> in 1851 and was named president for life in 1854.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-guatemala">Republic of Guatemala · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the nineteenth century its territory was a battleground for the wars of Central America: Guatemalan and Salvadoran armies installed or removed Honduran presidents, and at the <b>Battle of La Arada</b> in 1851 Rafael Carrera defeated a combined Honduran and Salvadoran army.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-honduras">Republic of Honduras · Military</a></li></ul></section>
